WEBVTT

cue-VID_U2_01-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Motivation is the reason why employees want to work hard and work effectively for the business. At Kestrel Ridge Sportswear, a sewing line should produce two hundred training tops a day. It manages one hundred and sixty. Absence runs at twelve percent; waste at eight. One question remains. What lifts effort: more pay, or a better job?

cue-VID_U2_01-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Maslow's hierarchy runs from physiological needs through safety, social, esteem and self-actualisation. The pyramid fills from the base. Pay and rest satisfy physiological needs. A contract and safe machines satisfy safety. A team gives social contact. Praise brings esteem. Mastering a skill reaches self-actualisation. Businesses can meet needs at each level, but the order is not rigid, so one reward rarely fits everyone.

cue-VID_U2_01-3
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Taylor linked pay to output through piece rates. A piece rate is an amount paid for each unit of output produced. A stopwatch times one seam; the standard is twenty tops an hour at forty cents each. At one hundred and sixty tops the operator earns sixty-four dollars. Work faster, earn more. Critics say quality can suffer.

cue-VID_U2_01-4
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Herzberg separated hygiene factors from motivators. Hygiene factors include pay, working conditions, security and company policy. They stop dissatisfaction but do not create lasting effort. Motivators include achievement, responsibility, recognition and personal growth. On the balance beam, extra pay lifts the left side briefly; adding responsibility and praise tips the right side down and keeps it there. The beam shows why money alone is not enough.

cue-VID_U2_01-5
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Financial rewards include time rates, piece rates, salaries, bonuses, commission, profit sharing and fringe benefits. Non-financial methods include job enrichment, job rotation, training, promotion, praise and employee of the month. Financial rewards can boost effort quickly, but may not create lasting commitment. Non-financial methods build responsibility and recognition at low cost. Kestrel Ridge Sportswear now tests both routes.

cue-VID_U2_01-6
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Because every operator stitches the same seam for eight hours, boredom grows and effort falls. Therefore Kestrel introduces job rotation across three stations and a daily praise board. So variety and recognition raise motivation, productivity improves and absence falls. That because, therefore, so chain is the reasoning examiners reward. Notice the mechanism: the job changes, the reward changes, and then the output changes.

cue-VID_U2_01-7
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Watch the eight-week trial. Output climbs from one hundred and sixty to two hundred and fifteen tops a day. Absence drops from twelve percent to seven percent. Waste falls from eight percent to four percent. Operators who exceed two hundred tops receive a thirty dollar bonus. At forty cents a top, two hundred and fifteen tops earn eighty-six dollars, plus the bonus. Counters move together.

cue-VID_U2_01-8
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Financial rewards act fast and are easy to measure, but they are costly and can damage quality. A bonus may push speed while seams fray. Non-financial rewards cost little and build responsibility, but results appear slowly and some workers dislike rotation. The best answer depends on the job and the evidence. For repetitive sewing, rotation plus recognition fits; for a sales team, commission may fit better. Neither method works alone.

cue-VID_U2_01-9
00:03:24.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Let us recap the chain. Motivation is the reason employees want to work hard. Maslow shows layered needs, Taylor links pay to output, and Herzberg separates hygiene from motivators. Kestrel Ridge Sportswear combined a forty cent piece rate, a thirty dollar bonus, rotation and praise. Output rose by fifty-five tops, absence fell by five percentage points, waste halved. For your answer, match the method to the job, quote one number from the data, and state one limitation. That turns description into evaluation.

